<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murzuq</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Murzuk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murzuq is an oasis town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. From the 16th century it was the capital of the Awlad Muhammad sultans of Fezzan and a major station on the caravan route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region. Its large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> fort dates from the Ottoman period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murzuq.jpg" alt="Murzuq" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franzfoto · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murzuq_-_Moschee_und_Festung_Qala_at_Turk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Murzuq, also spelled Murzuk, is an oasis town and the capital of the Murzuq District in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. It lies on the northern edge of the Murzuq Desert, an extremely arid region of ergs, or great sand dunes, that forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town developed around an oasis that served as a stop on the north-south trade route across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. From the 5th century BCE to the 5th century CE the area was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a>, a city state that operated the trans-Saharan routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and the states of West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>. By 1300 the Arab Banu Sulaym tribe ruled the area. By 1400 it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a>, whose legacy survives in street names in the Kanembu and Kanuri languages, and in the later 15th century it became a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>.</p><p>Early in the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-fasi">Muhammad al-Fasi</a> founded the Awlad Muhammad dynasty, which held power in the city until 1812. Tradition holds him to be a Moroccan sharif. His dynasty reinvigorated pilgrim traffic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and Murzuq became part of a slaving network reaching into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>. By the late 16th century it was more important than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadames">Ghadames</a>.</p><p>Under Ottoman rule, from 1574 to 1912, Murzuq was at times the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> and enjoyed a long period of prosperity. It had a major fort and was termed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. An Ottoman garrison was usually kept there, but local control stayed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Fezzan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the early nineteenth century Murzuq was the jumping-off point for British expeditions in search of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. The expedition of Denham, Oudney and Clapperton travelled there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripoli</a> in 1822. Many British explorers thought the town unhealthy: James Richardson, in 1846, reported that three quarters of the Europeans who came there fell ill with what was called Mourzuk fever.</p><p>The town declined as modern transport replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> to the Italians in 1912, though the town was not occupied until 1914. On 11 January 1941 about 70 men of the British Long Range Desert Group and a few Free French soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> raided the town and its small Italian airbase, and the Italian garrison in the old fort eventually surrendered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murzuk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He made <b>Murzuk</b> his capital, displacing earlier local rulers, and the dynasty soon acknowledged the overlordship of Ottoman Tripolitania.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-fezzan">Sultanate of Fezzan</a></li></ul></section>
